  "prompt": "[system]\nYou generate executable, reliable VRP metaheuristics for evolutionary search.\n\n[user]\nVERBAL GRADIENT MODE: follow the reflection below as the primary source of change.\n\nUse the supplied parents as supporting evidence only.\n\nPARENT-SPECIFIC LLM REFLECTIONS:\n\nParent cand_000008 LLM reflection:\n- Summary: Candidate regressed to 35.4% gap (vs parent 23.0%) because sorting by farthest depot first increased worst-case route imbalance.\n- Aim: Revert to deterministic sequential insertion plus a lightweight 2-opt or relocate local search to smooth max-route distances.\n\nVRP objective: lower maximum route distance across trucks is better. Feasibility is mandatory.\n\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000008: furthest_first_insertion\nStatus: valid; gap: 35.366\nThought: Replace customer order in greedy min-max insertion: instead of processing customers by index, sort them by descending distance from depot (furthest first). This aims to place challenging long-distance customers early, potentially reducing worst-case route distances.\nMean gap: 35.366044127274975\nMean max route distance: 2.768052077623105\nMean total route distance: 16.22405120487759\nGap by instance size: {'100': 35.366044127274975}\nGap by truck count: {'6': 35.366044127274975}\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: vrp:size:100\nHistory role: regime specialist\nCode:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef solve_vrp(distance_matrix, truck_count):\n    n = distance_matrix.shape[0]\n    customers = list(range(1, n))\n    # Sort customers by descending distance from depot\n    depot_distances = distance_matrix[0, customers]\n    sorted_indices = np.argsort(-depot_distances)  # descending\n    customers_sorted = [customers[i] for i in sorted_indices]\n    routes = [[0, 0] for _ in range(truck_count)]\n    route_distances = [0.0] * truck_count\n    for cust in customers_sorted:\n        best_new_max = float('inf')\n        best_new_route_d = float('inf')\n        best_route_idx = None\n        best_pos = None\n        for r_idx in range(truck_count):\n            route = routes[r_idx]\n            for pos in range(1, len(route)):\n                old_d = route_distances[r_idx]\n                removed = distance_matrix[route[pos-1], route[pos]]\n                added = distance_matrix[route[pos-1], cust] + distance_matrix[cust, route[pos]]\n                new_d = old_d - removed + added\n                # Compute new max route distance\n                other_max = max(route_distances[j] for j in range(truck_count) if j != r_idx) if truck_count > 1 else 0.0\n                new_max = max(new_d, other_max)\n                if new_max < best_new_max or (new_max == best_new_max and new_d < best_new_route_d):\n                    best_new_max = new_max\n                    best_new_route_d = new_d\n                    best_route_idx = r_idx\n                    best_pos = pos\n        routes[best_route_idx].insert(best_pos, cust)\n        route_distances[best_route_idx] = best_new_route_d\n    report_best_vrp(routes)\n    return routes\n```\n\nImplement exactly this interface:\n\ndef solve_vrp(\n    distance_matrix: np.ndarray,  # (n, n), depot is node 0\n    truck_count: int,\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n\nRules:\n- VRP is a static multi-truck routing problem; the objective is to minimize the maximum route distance across trucks.\n- Return exactly truck_count routes.\n- Each route must start at depot node 0 and end at depot node 0.\n- Every customer node 1..n-1 must appear exactly once across all routes.\n- An unused truck route must be [0, 0].\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates, instance size, truck count, or dataset details.\n- Keep all search and improvement loops finite and bounded by instance size; never use open-ended loops.\n- Call report_best_vrp(routes) whenever you find a better complete feasible route set, especially before expensive improvement loops.\n- Do not read/write files, use network, spawn subprocesses, or call external solvers.\n- Allowed imports only: numpy, math, random, heapq, itertools, collections, time.\n- No module-level mutable globals; the solver may be called many times across instances.\n\nInternal check before final JSON: correct solve_vrp signature, exactly truck_count routes,\nevery route starts and ends at 0, every customer appears exactly once, empty trucks use [0, 0],\nfinite instance-size-bounded loops, deterministic tie handling, report_best_vrp used for\nincumbents, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"short snake_case_or_title name\",\n  \"thought\": \"brief public summary of the construction, balancing, improvement, and tie-break logic\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}\n\nNo Markdown, fences, or text outside JSON. The code string must define solve_vrp.",
